  "worked_solution": "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=-2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(-2,2).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ at $(-2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(-2,2) & =(-2)^{2}(2)+2(-2)-(2) \\\\  & =8-4-2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(-2,2) & ={f}_{y}(-2,2)(y-2) \\\\ z-3 & =2(y-2) \\\\ z & =2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(-2,2,3)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-2 \\\\ z=2y-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x=-2,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=\u27e81,0,0\u27e9.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2y-1$ (or $2y-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=\u27e80,2,-1\u27e9.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =\u27e81,0,0\u27e9\\times \u27e80,2,-1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to $C$ at $P$ is $j+2\\,k.$",
